uld I discard to maximize my chances?\n\nIf I discard B7, then I have B8 and B9 left. If someone else discards a B7, I can't form the chow. But if I keep B7, I can form B7-B8-B9. Similarly, if I discard B8, I lose the possibility of forming that chow. So maybe keeping B7 and B8 is better.\n\nLooking at the W tiles: I have W4, W5, W6, W7, W9. If I discard W7, maybe someone can take it for a chow. But if I keep W7, I can form a chow with W5-W6-W7. But I need another tile. Alternatively, if I discard W9, maybe I can form a chow of W7-W8-W9 if I get W8. But W8 is in the unshown with 4 left. Maybe it's possible.\n\nHonor tiles: J1 and J3. If I discard J1, maybe someone can take it for a pung. But if I keep it, I can use it as a pair or part of a meld. Similarly for J3.\n\nThe T8 is in my hand. The previous player has a meld of T7-T8-T9, so T8 is already part of a chow. If I discard T8, maybe someone else can use it. But since the previous player already has a chow of T7-T8-T9, they might not need it. So maybe keeping T8 is safer.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles: For example, B8 has 4 left. I have two B8s. If I discard a B8, there are still 3 left, so it's possible someone else can take it. But if I keep it, I can form a pung if I get another B8. But since there are 4 in total, and I have two, there are two left. If I discard one, there are three left. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about the possible winning hands. Since I have a lot of W tiles, maybe forming a chow of W4-W5-W6 and another of W7-W8-W9 (if I can get W8). But I need to get W8. Alternatively, if I can form a pung of W7, but I have only one. The unshown W7 has 2 left. If I discard W7, someone might take it, but if I keep it, I can try to form a pung if another W7 comes up. But since I need to discard one tile, maybe discarding W7 is a good idea if I can get another one later.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discarding a tile that's not part of any possible meld. For example, T5 is in my hand. The next player has a T5 in their